    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_36dc04d8d7a9c0c53b02fa325d07ad93b91112e726ef277c2c2d0d9ee31d3aa3",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The exceptional-zero obstruction is converted from a question about one isolated L-function into leverage from relations across families. A hypothetical near-one Dirichlet zero repels comparable zeros and strengthens class-number bounds for other discriminants; conversely, eliminating it can be reduced to collective observables such as a sub-half normalized zeta gap or more than fifty-percent modular nonvanishing. Goldfeld's multiplicity mechanism and the Gross-Zagier triple-zero examples then motivate Iwaniec's engineered elliptic-curve family, whose forced central vanishing and coefficient combinations transfer the obstruction to family moments and cancellation estimates.",
